checks passed.\n\n**The defect:** `_landing_commit` (`intake.py:1837`) takes the oldest commit that touches the disposition spec as the commit that landed it. In a shallow repository, git treats the cut-off (\"boundary\") commit as a root, and a root counts as touching every file in its tree. So whenever the real landing is beyond the shallow window, the sweep judges the adoption in the wrong tree.\n\n**Reproduction:** I ran both directions myself, with the suite's own real-git fixtures and the actual bare sweep, after marking the repository shallow the way git records it:\n\n| Case | Full history | Shallow |\n|---|---|---|\n| Accepted adoption, successor then completes normally | landing found correctly, exit 0 | landing read as HEAD, refused \"names no open row\", exit 1 |\n| Refused adoption, newer tree shows the successor open | exit 1, WI-021 still needs WI-005 | exit 0, WI-021 re-pointed to WI-020 |\n\nIn the refused case I reopened the successor by hand. The reviewer reached the same overturn under a newer tree.\n\n**Why FIX:**\n- **Scope:** a shallow clone (`git clone --depth`, the default in most CI checkouts) is ordinary git state, not a tampered host. The kit already treats it as supported and refuses on short history instead of guessing: `baseline_snapshot._shallow_commits`, and the ruling-sync reader covered by TC-313.\n- **It breaks ruling 009** in both directions, and contradicts this round's own rule that an unknown landing refuses.\n- **The fix is small:** when the oldest touch is a shallow-boundary commit, the landing reader should answer \"unknown\" and fall through to the existing `_UNLANDED` refusal. The shallow set should come from one shared reader, not a second copy. Add one regression on a real shallow repository, and fold the wording into LLR-335/TC-360 at the spine checkpoint already owed.\n\nWith that change, short history becomes \"unknown\" instead of a usable commit, which should end this replay-eligibility class rather than prompting 